---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import ShiftCalculator from '../components/ShiftCalculator.astro';
import { PATTERNS } from '../lib/shiftPatterns';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is an "anchor date" and where do I find mine?',
    a: 'Your anchor date is simply the first day of any block of shifts you know you worked (or will work). Check last week in your work app, a posted roster, or a pay stub, and pick the first day of that run. ShiftCal repeats your rotation forward and backward from that single date.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does ShiftCal work for rotations that are not listed?',
    a: 'The six patterns here cover the most common US rotations (2-2-3 Panama, Pitman, DuPont, 4-on-4-off, 48/96, Kelly). If your department runs a local variant, pick the closest pattern and check the first two weeks against your posted roster before trusting the rest of the year.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my schedule data uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. Everything is calculated in your browser. ShiftCal has no accounts and no server-side storage; your dates never leave your device.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get the calendar onto my phone?',
    a: 'After generating your calendar, tap "Add to my phone calendar". This downloads a standard .ics file that opens in Apple Calendar, Google Calendar, or Outlook and adds each of your shifts as an all-day event for the next 12 months.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="ShiftCal — Shift Schedule Calculator for US Shift Workers"
  description="Free shift schedule calculator. Enter your rotation (2-2-3 Panama, Pitman, DuPont, 4-on-4-off, 48/96, Kelly) and one known workday to get your personal 12-month calendar with US holidays and paydays — exportable to your phone."
  h1="Am I working that day?"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    If you work a rotating schedule, "what days am I working next month?" should not require counting
    squares on a paper calendar. Pick your rotation below, enter <strong>one day you know you start a
    run of shifts</strong>, and ShiftCal builds your personal 12-month work calendar — with every US
    federal holiday answered (working or off), your biweekly paydays marked, and a one-tap export to
    your phone's calendar app.
  </p>

  <ShiftCalculator defaultPattern="all" />

  <h2>Which rotation do you work?</h2>
  <p>
    Every crew calls its schedule something different, but most US 24/7 operations run one of a
    handful of standard rotations. If you are not sure which one you are on, the shape of your week
    usually gives it away:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {PATTERNS.map((p) => (
      <li>
        <a href={p.path}>{p.name}</a> — {p.description}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each calculator page explains how that rotation actually works — cycle length, how many crews it
    takes, what your weekends look like, and the questions workers on that schedule ask most (like
    how many hours you really work per year, and what that means for overtime).
  </p>

  <h2>Why shift workers keep a 12-month view</h2>
  <p>
    A rotating schedule never lines up with the calendar everyone else lives on. Your family plans
    Thanksgiving in November; your rotation decided back in March whether you would be there. The
    long view is what lets you swap early instead of scrambling late: holidays you are scheduled to
    work show up months ahead, paydays land visibly next to your work stretches, and a vacation
    request can target the natural break in your cycle instead of burning extra leave days on either
    side of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    ShiftCal is deliberately simple: no login, no app install, nothing stored anywhere but your own
    browser. Set it up once, bookmark it, and the calculator remembers your rotation the next time
    you open the page. Firefighters can also jump straight to pre-configured calendars for
    <a href="/houston-fire-department-shift-calendar">Houston Fire</a>,
    <a href="/la-county-fire-shift-calendar">LA County Fire</a>, and
    <a href="/cal-fire-shift-calendar">CAL FIRE</a>, and anyone paid a night or weekend premium can
    check their real overtime rate with the
    <a href="/shift-differential-calculator">shift differential calculator</a>.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
